<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masada</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masada is a rock fortress on a plateau above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dead-sea">Dead Sea</a> in southern Israel, fortified by the Hasmoneans and rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> with palaces and storehouses. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a> it was the last rebel stronghold; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> its defenders killed themselves rather than surrender when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> breached the walls in 73 or 74 CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masada.jpg" alt="Masada" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godot13 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Israel-2013-Aerial_21-Masada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
